WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.173 --> 00:00:02.720
What if a microbe's identity could be read in light,

2
00:00:02.985 --> 00:00:04.421
and the evidence made visible?

3
00:00:04.683 --> 00:00:09.163
Mannose-coated gold nanoparticles attach to microbial surfaces,

4
00:00:09.359 --> 00:00:11.293
strengthening their Raman signals.

5
00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:15.539
Researchers compared nanoparticle chemistry and three laser wavelengths

6
00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:19.626
across fourteen microbial species, including two yeasts.

7
00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:23.669
At five hundred thirty-two nanometers, the optimized system reported

8
00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:28.404
ninety-six point one percent classification accuracy on held-out spectra.

9
00:00:28.678 --> 00:00:33.227
Explainable AI highlighted five influential Raman shifts,

10
00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.197
turning complex signals into species-specific spectral barcodes.

11
00:00:37.482 --> 00:00:40.272
These results come from cultured organisms.

12
00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:44.671
Clinical samples and broader pathogen diversity still need testing.

13
00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:47.333
Target the microbe. Strengthen the signal.

14
00:00:47.564 --> 00:00:49.625
Reveal the features behind the prediction.
